@timokau 

> Also I'd like to keep the `extraConfig` escape hatch.

I'd rather not if possible, because then we have problem of not being able to merge values set in `extraConfig` with all other values, there are essentially two incompatible ways of specifying configuration then. I didn't think of this earlier (I'll add it to the RFC), but with a `config` option, you can even do this which alleviates the need for `extraConfig` (for formats we have a parser for at least):
```nix
{
  services.foo.config = builtins.fromJSON ''
    {
      "logDir": "/var/log/foo"
      # ...
    }
  '';
}
```

If the user really doesn't want to use the modular `config`, what I suggest instead is to have a `configFile` option as an escape hatch, which when set, overrides the file generated by the `config` option. This also has the advantage that the user can just set that option to something stateful if they wish, like `"/var/lib/foo/config.json"` and manage it outside of NixOS.

While I like the idea, and even wanted to propose something similar before, I *think* this `extraConfig` should be part of a package, rather then NixOS module system. Each package should contain knowledge about it's configuration files.
```
postgresql = callPackage ( mkDerivation {
   name = "postgresql-XXX";
   ... blabla ...
   passthru.configuration = { this, lib, ...}: let cfg = this.configuration; in {
     options."postgresql.conf" = ... (each of these has options text, source, path)
     options."pg_hba.conf" = ...
     options."pg_ident.conf" = ...
     options."recovery.conf" = 
     config = {
       "postgresql.conf" = lib.mkDefault ''
         pg_hba = ${cfg."pg_hba.conf".path}
         pg_ident = ${cfg."pg_ident.conf".path}
       '';
       shellExec.postgres = "${this}/bin/postgres -c ${cfg."postgresql.conf".path}"
     };
   };
}) {};
```
So this knowledge module only describes which configuration files are supported by package and what is relationship between them. At the same time, it is possible to override any of these configuration files from NixOS configuration, as `pkgs.postgresql.configuration` is inserted into module system as a submodule of `services.postgresql`.

So this separates configuration between package and nixos - package defines internal resources, while NixOS defines interoperation between parts of system.

Note, that this doesn't mention service manager, the `shellExec.postgres` is also optional.

@Infinisil Regarding the ability to "steal" a config file, it *is* useful to have a `configFile` option to build the config file separately. But IMHO it shouldn't be overridable by the user. I.e. it's strictly a computed value like the `system.build.*` derivations. (This could also be implemented using a `.apply` attribute on the option, so building the `service.foo.settings` attribute would give you the config file in the Nix store. OTOH `.apply` is a bit questionable semantically...)

> Set it to a stateful location like /var/lib/foo/config.json if needed

While this is sometimes useful as an escape hatch, it also completely breaks reproducibility so we shouldn't get into the habit of providing this functionality.

From a discussion with @tilpner on IRC, who raised concerns about not having a `configText` option, I'll give a short summary:

- Argument: `configText` is convenient because you can just `configText = "the text"`, whereas with `configFile` it's `configFile = pkgs.writeText "foo" "the text"`. Counterargument: I don't think it's worth having to deal with another option, and a stringly typed one at that, when we can just mention in the manual how users can use `pkgs.writeText`.
- `configFile` can still do config checking by using the `apply` attribute of `mkOption` like so:
  ```nix
  mkOption {
    apply = path:
      # To allow stateful paths like "/var/lib/foo/config"
      if isString path then path
      else runCommand "file-checked" {
        nativeBuildInputs = [ foo ];
      } ''
        foo-check ${path}
        ln -s ${path} $out
      ''
  }
  ```
  Edit: It's probably better to just use `cp` instead of `ln -s`
- The replacement for `configText = readFile ./foo.conf` is `configFile = ./foo.conf`

Something I've wanted to discuss too: The type of such options is actually tied to the string generator function, so it might make sense for the types themselves to export the generator functions like `types.json.generate`, usable like:
```nix
{ options, lib, config, ... }: {
  options.foo.settings = lib.mkOption {
    type = lib.types.settings.json;
  };

  config.environment.etc."foo.json".text =
    options.foo.settings.type.generate config.foo.settings;
}
```

Or perhaps something like `lib.settings.json.{type,generate}` would be cleaner (a combination of `lib.types.json` and `lib.generators.toJSON`), usable like this:
```nix
{ lib, config, ... }:
let settingsMeta = lib.settings.json;
{
  options.foo.settings = lib.mkOption {
    inherit (settingsMeta) type;
  };
  
  config.environment.etc."foo.json".text = settingsMeta.generate config.foo.settings;
}
```

Ideally we would even write types and generators in the same code (perhaps by adding type checking to `lib.generators`), so that all cases (different types, nestings, lists) are handled by both, but that would conflate different concepts, so I'm not sure.

## Infinite recursion problem with self-referential `attrsOf` options

On IRC, @hyperfekt encountered a case of infinite recursion when trying to use this approach to define a module. Here's a simplified version of the module which you can `nix-instantiate --eval` to get the inf rec:
```nix
with import <nixpkgs/lib>;

(evalModules {
  modules = [
    ({ config, ... }: {
      options.settings = mkOption {
        type = types.attrsOf types.bool;
        default = {};
      };

      config.settings.foo = config.settings ? bar;
    })
  ];
}).config.settings.foo
```

In essence, the value of `settings.foo` should be equal to whether there is a `settings.bar` defined. This is a reasonable thing to want in order to set values depending on other values. Why this gives infinite recursion can be hard to figure out, so here's an explanation from a step-by-step evaluation perspective:
1. To know the value of `foo` we need to evaluate `config.settings ? bar`
2. To know the value of `config.settings ? bar` we need to know *all* attribute names of `config.settings`, because Nix is strict in all of them
3. To know all attribute names of `config.settings`, we need to know whether `foo` is present too
4. To know whether `foo` is present, it's *not enough* to just look at `foo` being defined in the module, because in case you have `foo = mkIf false ...`, foo won't be there!
5. So to know whether there's `mkIf`'s or so, the module needs to evaluate the *value* of `foo`
6. Go to 1

So that kind of sucks, but there are three ways I know of to get around this

## Solution 1: lazy `attrsOf`

The source of the problem is really that `attrsOf` evaluates attribute values to check for `mkIf` and such. So how about getting rid of a reliable `mkIf` but get this working in return. I originally saw this in @oxij's https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/pull/57123 where they introduced `lazyAttrsOf`, but I kind of brushed it off there because it wasn't beneficial there. In this case however I feel like it is not unreasonable to use. With the draft PR https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/pull/70138 introducing `lazyAttrsOf`, it's possible to write this functioning module:
```nix
with import ./lib;

(evalModules {
  modules = [
    ({ config, ... }: {
      options.settings = mkOption {
        type = types.lazyAttrsOf types.bool;
        default = {};
      };

      config.settings.foo = config.settings ? bar;
    })
  ];
}).config.settings.foo
```

Disadvantages:
- Even with `mkIf`, an attribute would still be present, while trying to evaluate its value throws an error that it's not defined. As an example, with `      config.settings.bar = mkIf false true;`, the above module will evaluate to `true`, even though `bar` was defined with a `mkIf false` which usually would have no influence on the result.

### Solution 2: Using `apply`

Since `mkOption`'s `apply` completely goes around the module system, it's possible to use it to change the final value of options freely without having to worry about it feeding back into the fixed point:
```nix
with import <nixpkgs/lib>;

(evalModules {
  modules = [
    ({ config, ... }: {
      options.settings = mkOption {
        type = types.attrsOf types.bool;
        default = {};
        apply = value: { foo = value ? bar; } // value;
      };
    })
  ];
}).config.settings.foo
```

Disadvantages:
- Only the module that defines this option can make such self-referential changes
- The module system can't be used to merge values in the `apply` function, so no `mkIf` and such, since it's just a raw function without any post processing. Well it's still possible to use the module system, but it would be rather ugly.

### Solution 3: Not exposing the changes

The problem can be circumvented by not feeding the changes back into the module system, but making the changes locally in the module, this can look like this:
```nix
with import <nixpkgs/lib>;

(evalModules {
  modules = [
    ({ config, ... }:
      let settings' = { foo = config.settings ? bar; } // config.settings;
      in {
      options.settings = mkOption {
        type = types.attrsOf types.bool;
        default = {};
      };
      options.foo = mkOption {
        default = settings'.foo;
      };
    })
  ];
}).config.foo
```

Disadvantages:
- The changes aren't exposed outside the module, meaning other modules can see inconsistent values for the option
- Only the module that defines this option can make such self-referential changes that will be applied
- The module system can't be used to merge values

## ToDo

I'll add this to the RFC contents (and later to the NixOS docs once merged) to document that this can occur. I think it's reasonable to introduce `lazyAttrsOf` as part of this RFC, so I'll make it part of it.

There's one implementation details (which I mentioned in https://github.com/NixOS/rfcs/pull/42#issuecomment-519750193) that might be nice to use for this. The idea is that assignments to specialized options have the same exact behavior as assignments to `settings`. In the current version of this RFC this is not the case, since I specified that any value in `settings` overrides specialized options for backwards compat. However it is possible to implement this without much trouble, here is an example:
<details>

```nix
with import <nixpkgs/lib>;
evalModules {
  modules = [
    ({ lib, pkgs, config, options, ... }:
      let
        cfg = config.services.hello;
      in {

        options.services.hello = {

          enable = lib.mkEnableOption "hello service";

          settings = lib.mkOption {
            type = types.attrsOf (types.either types.str types.bool);
            default = {};
          };

          greeting = lib.mkOption {
            type = types.str;
            apply = lib.const cfg.settings.GREETING;
            # Could introduce alias ignoreDefinitions
            #apply = lib.ignoringDefinitions cfg.settings.GREETING;
          };

        };

        config = lib.mkIf cfg.enable {
          services.hello.settings.GREETING = lib.modules.mkAliasAndWrapDefsWithPriority id options.services.hello.greeting;
          # Could introduce alias copyDefinitions
          #services.hello.settings.GREETING = lib.copyDefinitions id options.services.hello.greeting;
        };

      })
    {
      services.hello = {
        enable = true;
        # Both of these can be used without one overriding the other. For
        # conflicting definitions an error is thrown
        greeting = "Hello!";
        settings.GREETING = "Hello!";
      };
    }
  ];
}
```
</details>

While this has very nice behavior, the disadvantage is slightly worse UX: Because the specialized options acts as an alias to the general one, all potential errors are reported as if they were set from the general option, even ones where the specialized option was used. This doesn't sound too bad though.

The approach with `mkDefault` mentioned in this RFC right now has the other disadvantage that values in specialized options get ignored if it's set in `settings`, which is worse imo.

So I think I want to change this RFC to use this other approach

---

Other than that there's also the discussions:
- Pretty printing for config files -> Resolution is to not worry about pretty printing for now, just use any config generator available
- The problem I mentioned in https://github.com/NixOS/rfcs/pull/42#issuecomment-536747403 -> As a resolution I want to write some recommendations for how to get around this in the RFC

And also some slightly hotter topics:
- Should this RFC define guidelines for a `configFile` option? Should this allow stateful file paths? Perhaps it's best to just not go into `configFile` in this RFC to make it shorter
- Part 2 of this RFC in general might still be slightly controversial, but overall I think it's a good idea, e.g. see https://github.com/NixOS/rfcs/pull/42#issuecomment-541228925

@Infinisil: 
> If the user really doesn't want to use the modular config, what I suggest instead is to have a configFile option as an escape hatch, which when set, overrides the file generated by the config option. This also has the advantage that the user can just set that option to something stateful if they wish, like "/var/lib/foo/config.json" and manage it outside of NixOS.

Maybe it would be possible to have this "escape hatch" also be standardized with the new `settings` option? If the user could just set `configFile` and override options that may have been set in other modules, this could lead to a lot of problems. Also, developers of modules might otherwise choose not to provide this option, which wouldn't be great for standardization.

I envision something like the following interface:

#### Case 1
```nix
{
  module.settings.textual = "...";
}
```
Works as expected with the configuration set to the textual value.

#### Case 2
```nix
{
  module.settings.textual = "...";
  module.settings.foo = 10;
}
```
Merge error.

#### Case 3
```nix
{
  module.settings.textual = lib.mkForce "...";
  module.settings.foo = 10;
}
```
Overrides the value of foo.

#### Case 4 (maybe, likely not a great idea)
```nix
{
  module.settings.prependTextual = "...";
  module.settings.foo = 10;
  module.settings.appendTextual = "...";
}
```
Works as expected.

### Advantages

* Standardized way to set configurations from text files
  * Improved compatibility with existing configurations
* Possible way to express unsuitable configuration formats in a standardized manner (maybe out of scope for this RFC)

### Drawbacks

The problem with this design is that it would introduce special handling for some values of `settings`, e.g. it would be non-obvious how to actually place the option `textual` into a configuration file.

Also, inexperienced users might be able to abuse this option in some way.

I hope my suggestions are useful and understandable, thanks for your work on this RFC.

Well actually I guess the current `(pkgs.formats.<..> {}.generator` functions just take a `settings` value and emit a file. You'd have to append to that file during build/runtime which is a bit trickier, but still doable. E.g. with
```nix
let
  pkgs = import <nixpkgs> {};
  format = pkgs.formats.toml {};
  file = format.generate "file.toml" { foo = "foo"; };
  extraConfig = ''
    bar = "bar"
  '';
  finalFile = pkgs.runCommandNoCC "final-file.toml" {
    inherit extraConfig;
    passAsFile = [ "extraConfig" ];
  } ''
    cat ${file} $extraConfigPath > $out
  '';
in finalFile
```

The problem is that once any single module in nixpkgs uses `settings`, every user is forced to migrate to it, `extraConfig` can't be used anymore, because both can't be set. And in fact this is the case for pretty much all `settings` modules, because `settings` defaults are defined like
```nix
{
  config.services.foo.settings = {
    port = mkDefault ...;
 };
}
```

@lheckemann Thanks for your context!

A `lib.unset` seems like a good idea, though what I finally figured out below isn't bad neither: it makes it very clear how to group conditions together. What I would like though is an example in this RFC of the "blessed" way...  /cc @Infinisil 

```nix
{
    quantumModule.k3s.settings = {

      write-kubeconfig-mode = "0400";
      kube-apiserver-arg = [
        "api-audiences=api,spire-server"
        "feature-gates=EphemeralContainers=true"
      ];
      # TODO: switch when calico CNI is operational
      # flannel-backend = "none";
      # TODO: switch when calico BGP service advisory is operational
      # disable = ["servicelb"];

    } // lib.attrsets.optionalAttrs (cfg.role == "agent") {

      token-file = "/etc/rancher/k3s/joinToken";

    } // lib.attrsets.optionalAttrs (cfg.role == "server") {


    };
}
```

... might maybe loose clarity once options are nested? or does `//` do "deep merges"?

